{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfZVgkAu6xvr5w5pjbgx5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've written the candidate, and it passes the self-check: its smoke-fit ELPD-LOO is −1014.4, against −1008.9 for the current best model. That smoke fit was short (100 draws, one chain). Admission still has to check it for novelty, convergence and speed.\n\n**Model name:** `bayesian_generator_comparison_personal_switch`\n\n**Hypothesis:** People judge randomness the normative way, by Bayesian model comparison. A sequence looks random to the extent that a fair coin explains it better than two generators people might suspect:\n- a **biased coin** with unknown bias, which explains unbalanced H/T counts;\n- a **sticky or switchy coin** with unknown switch rate, which explains long runs or rigid alternation.\n\nThe one distortion is that each person's idea of a fair coin switches between H and T at their own rate, not exactly half the time. Those personal rates come from a population distribution.\n\n**Why this, given the critique:** The best model, `personal_ideal_alternation`, fails on two checks:\n- **Balance:** with alternation rates matched, people prefer the more balanced sequence. Here the biased-coin generator explains unbalanced sequences well, so they look less random.\n- **Long runs:** people penalise long runs more than alternation rate alone predicts. The sticky-coin generator's share of the comparison should penalise runs somewhat, but the model has no separate longest-run term, so that gap may not close fully.\n\nBecause people's fair-coin switch rates differ, the model keeps the individual differences in liking alternation that make the current best model strong. It differs from `personal_switch_belief`, which only scores how likely a sequence is under the personal fair coin: this model also subtracts how well the other two generators explain it.\n\n**Parameters:** a population mean and spread for the personal switch rate (set up so the sampler handles it well), the per-person rates, and one sensitivity parameter. The model reads `participant_id` from the CSV, like the current best model does.\n\nOne fix was needed: `pm.math.log1p` doesn't exist, so I replaced it with `log(1 − q)`.\n\nFiles are in `experiment1/model_loop/iter_1/candidate_1/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1020,"cache_read_input_tokens":52234,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1020,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":641,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"acf6061b-5c7b-4f89-9983-738593302eaa","uuid":"e4120f80-a04f-4980-85c5-80fb05a140ed","timestamp":"2026-09-30T10:50:45.459Z","request_id":"req_011CfZVgj98U5djJmss2uauJ"}
